    "language": [],
    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Bacterial genomes are intricately augmented by the integration of prophages, which play a role in bacterial evolution, including among nosocomial pathogens. This work asks whether prophage presence does or does not impact bacterial adaptability and antimicrobial resistance (AMR) mechanisms, utilizing genomic and phenotypic analyses to explore the genetic diversification driven by prophage integration. Our results highlight a significant increase in bacterial resistance linked to prophage elements, suggesting novel insights into the dynamics of AMR development. Through computational and phenotypic study, this work not only investigates the complex interplay between prophages and bacterial genomes but also proposes the potential of targeting prophage components in designing effective antimicrobial strategies using comprehensive genomic tools. These findings advocate for a deeper integration of genomic approaches in combatting resistant infections.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Chang, T. and Bollyky, P. (2024). Temperate Phage Infection and its Associations with Antimicrobial Resistance: A Genomic and Phenotypic Study . Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/wh246bt7387. https://doi.org/10.25740/wh246bt7387.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
